<section aria-labelledby="braking-experiment-heading" class="braking-experiment">
  <h2 id="braking-experiment-heading">Single braking experiment</h2>
  <canvas
    id="experiment-canvas"
    class="convoy-canvas"
    role="img"
    aria-label="A line of 20 cars on a straight road; the leader is ringed in blue"
    width="900"
    height="220"
  ></canvas>
  <button
    type="button"
    id="experiment-tap-button"
    class="brake-tap-button"
    aria-label="Tap the brakes (single braking experiment)"
  >
    Tap the brakes
  </button>
  <p>
    Tap the brakes on the lead car and watch what happens to the line behind it. The lead car
    slows only briefly and only a little — but by the time the second car notices, reacts, and
    brakes in turn, it has less room and less time to recover than the car ahead did. Every
    driver takes a fraction of a second, their reaction time, to notice a change and respond to
    it. During that delay the gap keeps closing, so the driver ends up braking harder than the
    situation strictly required, just to avoid running into the car ahead. That harder braking
    becomes the next car's problem, magnified again by its own reaction time and its own
    following distance. The disturbance doesn't fade as it travels backward through the line; it
    grows, hop by hop, until cars several places back are braking far harder than the lead car
    ever did, some right down to a stop. Watch the labels above each car: it "notices late" once
    the car ahead has already reacted; it's "braking harder" once its own deceleration exceeds
    the car ahead's; and it's "recovering" once it finally starts speeding back up. No collision
    caused this, no traffic light forced it, and no obstacle blocked the road. Reaction time and
    following distance, alone, were enough.
  </p>
</section>
